{"kind":"report","text":"Finished the focused I2 integration preparation requested in 5f7dbd8c, for the existing full commission efb4e323. This is a bounded static handoff at approved I1 a3e5a77e, R1v11 996c3e58, R2v14 4b3bf5da and R4v9 85be9f0b. It is not an ordinary verdict on the newly arrived I2 candidate744f2447 or newer sibling drafts. No new P2 or implementation commission is established by this preparation.\n\n1. Preserve three distinct admission paths. After U hands an accepted commitment to V with no current hold and unchanged terms, U's new report, renewal and manifest proposal fail the current-performer guard. A hold that merely expired leaves U responsible. A late signed check remains a new act requiring the current change.check grant, named checker and exact job tree/configuration. A transport result instead proves the original request FactRef/ordinal, addressed incarnation and actual deciding entry. An outside operation's answer must authenticate its own numbered attempt before trusted outcome admission; judgeOutcome currently carries evidence without authenticating it. There is no outside-outcome ingress in current Core. Handover must neither erase admitted duties nor turn old performer status into an authority exemption. Anchors: R2 1978–2064,1201–1202,2159–2163; source delivery.ts186–208, settle.ts65–83, core.ts223–235 for signed-act grant checking.\n\n2. Preserve deciding facts and pre-entry conditional effects. Timeout then first authentic late answer may replace the timeout's deciding fact. Retry creates a new job and supersedes the old one; a later old-job answer records history without changing the new job or old deciding fact. Evaluate every if/unless against pre-entry state: otherwise the preceding state effect in check/check-error makes the decidedBy condition false. The timed and secondary full-fact self extension is needed with that rule; current validate.ts482–484 allows self only for a nascent primary item reference. Use timeout→late-answer and timeout→retry→old-answer as one compact paired family. Anchors: R2 1201–1206,2232–2269,2311–2354; R1 2323–2330,6878–6909.\n\n3. A settlement reservation does not waive authority or binding. Preserve both publication/withdrawal arrival orders and the same final state with one receipt. A receipt may still be recorded after finality without reopening the item. The first correctly bound higher license request with decided\u003ck\u003c=3 may consume its particular reserved decision entry on applied or expressly covered refusal; an identical envelope reuses the earlier decision. A different envelope reusing a decided number, request1 or unbound identifiers is new work. Unknown dispatch cannot send a synthetic settlement. Wider malformed-field/no-item refusal coverage still requires the common sibling agreement; do not infer it from historical R3. Anchors: R2 2440–2669,4109–4136,4163–4169; R1 2702–2749,2803–2815,3384–3426; R4 F27.\n\n4. Extend the shared grammar and its closure accounting together. Current declaration/validator support is strict and lacks the proposed settles, capability, additional-subject, receive-opening/copy, record, detached-text, nested-fact, conditional-effect, fanout and collect forms. Unsupported definitions must remain refused until their agreed forms and closures exist. Current defaults are12 item types,48 acts,3 additional subjects,8 declared sends per act and32 total sends per entry; these are distinct configurable limits, not one send quota. Proposed issue/change definitions need12/14 types,50/52 acts and4 additional subjects. Current reserve.ts39–74 accounts for entries only, with finite timed/request/operation/closing-checkpoint duties and conservative clause closure. R1's permission to reserve more in that dimension does not implement items, records, retained bytes and pending-request dimensions. Anchors: validator143–151,179,573–618,632–646,657,698; bounds.ts; R1§17.2 6050–6089; I1 delta529–535.\n\n5. Preserve expanded-send correlation, including skipped sends and each-subject identity. Current sends.ts57 assigns an ordinal per declared form and frame.ts281 selects the clause by that ordinal. Adding fanout without reconstructing the exact expanded send/form/element mapping misidentifies later clauses. R2 publication sends links0–31,unpin32,index33,result34:35 sends. R1's companion/witness gives34 sends/result33. Both have33 requests and the declared67-entry publication closure, because the index is advisory. Equal request/entry counts do not resolve differing ordinals. The non-staged-here case and conditional omission of unpin must be covered; no skipped send takes an ordinal. Common owner agreement remains required before dependent source. Anchors: R2 3878–3963; R1§6.6/18.6; source sends.ts19–85 and frame.ts239–300.\n\nArchitecture: retain one validator, shared derivation, send/result identities and reservation model for admission and replay. I2 owns canonical definition data, handlers/settles and agreed grammar integration. cc570904 owns common five-dimensional capacity; d56d7f65 owns153–157 and sibling convergence, including ordinals and wider G15. I3 owns capability/destination/checker authentication and platform settlement; IA owns execution/outside-answer custody. No extra lane engine or implicit activation of refusing production ports.\n\nSecurity: capability, grant/state guards and authenticated exact obligation binding still apply at full capacity. Old tokens, unselected forks and unknown attempts retain their own identities and cleanup duties after hold end or handover. Result history, responsibility, selected current evidence and outside settlement must not be conflated.\n\nSimplification: carry the above paired traces through the existing real store/turn/client boundary and controlled scheduler. Useful distinctions include reserved first job answer versus further answer, final publication closure versus intermediate unknown/committed update, existing closes-copy decision versus unrelated refusal, a correctly bound license decision versus an unbound/reused number, and closing checkpoint alone versus one beside pending duties. These are review inputs, not per-field suites, fixed test counts or additional gates. Full I2 removal, guides, all ordinary evidence, connected browser/device/publication outcomes and highest-priority useful-test economy remain owed.\n\nCoverage: n2 completed relevant R2 §§3.3/4.3/6/7.2/7.4/13.6/14.2 and targeted complete definition rows; R1 §§4.3/6.11/7.4/11.9/18.16 and conditional paragraphs; R4 relevant proof/fault rows. Fresh full source definition/ports/core/delivery/settle/timed plus bounded judge/frame/effects/validator contexts. N3 freshly read complete declaration/bounds/validator/reserve/sends and bounded current turn/state/frame/delivery/delta/common capacity/form/ordinal contexts. Its13-subject ledger /tmp/n3-i2-reserve-forms-preparation-ledger.json SHA256de70afd07924afef1e6d06ee0262778586226e8b18458636c1638cd276d7b835 matches checker's exact source/design bytes/LF/SHA. Checker independently read complete R2 responsibility/attempt and check authority contexts, check rows, publication ordinals, R1 pre-entry condition paragraphs and source-impact rows, current whole reserve/sends/bounds and bounded clause/result/outcome/validator/grant contexts. All claimed truncations recovered. This is bounded preparation, not fresh whole-package acceptance or execution evidence.\n\nNo tests/build/install/typecheck, repository imports/scripts, private diagnostics, evaluator/probe/provider effects, source edits, merge or deployment. Six private script bodies remain unopened. The newer I2/R1/R3/C2 invitations now available require their own exact-head ordinary reviews.","body":{"finished":"true","head":"a3e5a77eb28e91e1a0458af0526eea446387b7c4","scope":"Bounded static I2 integration preparation; not verdict on newer candidate744f2447 or sibling drafts"}}